<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="recitation-Vector-Spaces">
  <title>Vector Spaces</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      A <term>vector space</term> is a set of objects (called <term>vectors</term>) which can be added together and scaled (i.e., multiplied by a real number, called a <term>scalar</term>) while remaining the same type of object.
      Vector spaces are a mathematical structure of study in Linear Algebra (in which you would learn a more general definition, but this will suffice for us).
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <page>
    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the set <m>\mathcal{M}_{m\times n}</m> consisting of all <m>m\times n</m> matrices with real number entries.
          If <m>A, B</m> are two such matrices, is <m>A + B</m> also? What about <m>\alpha A</m> for some value <m>\alpha \in \R</m>? Is <m>\mathcal{M}_{m\times n}</m> a vector space?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\mathcal{P}</m> be the set of all polynomials with real number coefficients.
          Is <m>\mathcal{P}</m> a vector space? What about <m>\mathcal{P}_n</m>, the subset consisting only of polynomials of degree up to <m>n</m>? (What about <m>\mathcal{P}_n'</m>, the subset consisting only of polynomials of <em>exactly</em> degree <m>n</m>?)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is the set <m>\Q</m> of rational numbers a vector space? What about the set <m>\mathbb{I}</m> of irrational numbers?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>


  <page>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Consider a homogeneous differential equation of the form <m>y'' + p(x) y' + q(x) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </introduction>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Is the set <m>S</m>, consisting of all functions <m>y(x)</m> which are solutions to the differential equation, a vector space?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>


      <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            What about solution set <m>T</m> for the nonhomogeneous equation <m>y'' + p(x) y' + q(x) = f(x)</m>?
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>A</m> be a given <m>n \times n</m> matrix and <m>\lambda</m> a given real number.
          Consider the equation <m>A \vec{x} = \lambda \vec{x}</m>, and the set <m>\mathcal{E}(A)</m> consisting of all vectors <m>\vec{x}</m> which satisfy this equation.
          Is <m>\mathcal{E}(A)</m> a vector space?
        </p>

        <p>
          [Note: *foreshadowing*.]
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>
</worksheet>